# .github-guard — the declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
#
# git-config format: check it with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ; before you trust it.
#
# github-guard's `github-protect-main` pre-commit hook reads THIS FILE FROM THE
# DEFAULT BRANCH ON THE SERVER — never the working tree — and reconciles
# `main`'s branch protection with it. Reading the server copy is what stops a
# contributor's branch from unprotecting the branch merely by being checked out
# while someone commits.

# Required status checks for main. A declaration beats discovery exactly.
#
# WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND THE THREE THINGS THAT WERE WRONG WITH IT (#278).
#
# Until this file existed the required list was whatever had been typed into
# the settings page, and nothing in the repository knew what it said. It had
# drifted into all three failure modes at once:
#
#   1. `unit tests` was required and is NOT the unit tests. It is the job id
#      `asan` in ci.yml — an AddressSanitizer smoke build on the NIGHTLY
#      toolchain — wearing `name: unit tests`. The unit tests are the `test`
#      matrix, below. A reviewer reading a green `unit tests` tick was reading
#      a tick that says nothing about `cargo test --lib`.
#
#      The old job-level `continue-on-error: true` also hid sanitizer
#      findings. It was removed in #303 when the nightly was pinned. Only
#      the toolchain-install step now tolerates failure; a sanitizer finding
#      makes the job red. tests/ci_profile.rs treats a JOB carrying
#      `continue-on-error` as non-gating, but `asan` no longer carries it.
#      Step-level tolerance does not change the job's result.
#
#      ASan remains advisory by decision: a pinned nightly can still break
#      independently of stable, while a red ASan job is visible to reviewers.
#      So `unit tests under ASan (nightly toolchain, advisory)` is deliberately
#      ABSENT from the list below. Declaring the set is the ONLY
#      way to remove a required check — discovery is additive by construction
#      and would keep re-adding it — which is why this file, and not a click
#      in the settings page, is the fix.
#
#   2. `test-ubuntu-24.04-arm` is produced on EVERY run and was required on
#      none of them. It is not a cross-check of the other legs: `c_char` is
#      `u8` on aarch64 Linux and `i8` on x86_64 and macOS, so a lint about a
#      C-ABI pointer cast fails on one architecture and passes on the other
#      (#268, ci.yml:22-24). That is precisely a failure the other two legs
#      cannot see, and until now it could be merged past. It gates through
#      ci-ok, which needs the whole `test` matrix.
#
#   3. Nothing was checked against the workflow at all. Discovery requires
#      checks BY JOB NAME, which is only ever a guess at the PR gate, and it
#      drifts silently: a renamed job, an added workflow, or any future check
#      reporting under the github-actions app changes what is required without
#      anyone deciding to. Pinning it costs nothing and makes the intent
#      reviewable in a diff.
#
# `ci-ok` IS NOW THE ONLY REQUIRED CHECK (#409). It needs every gating job --
# the test matrix, the Windows-authored fixtures, the full suite, the change
# filter, the Windows chkdsk verdict, the CLI and semver -- and fails on a
# failed, cancelled, missing or unexpectedly skipped dependency
# (scripts/ci-verdict.sh). ASan remains advisory. The five direct checks that
# sat beside it during the rollout are gone: ci-ok has reported on main since,
# and naming jobs here is what strands every merge when one is renamed --
# GitHub reads a required check that never reports as pending forever.
#
# WHAT KEEPS THIS TRUE: `chore check:ci-gate` (run in CI's ubuntu test leg and
# by `chore lint`) runs rust-fs-core's ci-gate. It fails if a job in ci.yml is
# neither in ci-ok's needs nor declared non-gating, and if this file names
# anything but `ci-ok`.
#
# A workflow-level path filter would suppress every required context and leave
# protection pending forever; keep the workflow itself running on all PRs.
#
# The name must match the check-run name GitHub reports, which is the job's
# `name:` -- `ci-ok`.
[checks]
	required = ci-ok
